{"reference":"Jeremiah 14","book":"Jeremiah","chapter":14,"status":"pending","sections":[{"kind":"interpretive_questions","title":"Reading catastrophe","blocks":[{"id":"block_1","text":"One interpretive approach reads the chapter’s drought, empty water jars, hunger, tears, wounds, unburied bodies, and threatened displacement as a sustained portrayal of catastrophe and survival. The accumulation of bodily, agricultural, and civic distress keeps the crisis from becoming an abstraction: it is seen in cracked ground, failing animals, grieving Jerusalem, and a people who seek relief while confronting judgment.","verse_refs":["Jeremiah 14:1-22"],"confidence":"high","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_014_54d6fbb27cd81eb2577b4fe6"]}]},{"kind":"surrounding_passages","title":"The wider book","blocks":[{"id":"block_2","text":"The received book of Jeremiah brings together varied forms, including poetry, prose sermons, narratives, letters, headings, repeated accounts, restoration collections, oracles concerning nations, and an appendix. A practical outline places this chapter within the large opening collection of chapters 2–25, while leaving questions about the book’s longer composition and textual history open.","confidence":"medium","interpretation_level":"disputed","provenance_refs":["reader_path_jeremiah_014_4302de4a951c0ce902664489"]}]}],"generated_metadata":null}